      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To evaluate the adequacy of pre-operative analgesia and patient's satisfaction at the accident and emergency department, in terms of pain relief."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "Observational study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PLACE AND DURATION OF STUDY",
          "content": "The Accident and Emergency Department (A&E) of Civil Hospital, Karachi, during April and May, 2007."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODOLOGY",
          "content": "Patients presenting with acute abdomen or tauma were included. Visual Analogue Scale (VAS) was used to grade severity of pain. Pain at presentation, analgesic prescribed, post-analgesic residual pain and patient satisfaction were assessed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "A total of 166 patients (109 males and 57 females) were enrolled in the study. It included 98 (59%) cases of trauma and 68 (41%) patients with acute abdomen. Mean pain score was 6.95+/-2.03 on VAS. Female patients (p=0.008) and patients with acute abdomen (p<0.001) experienced significantly more pain as compared to male and trauma patients respectively. Analgesics were prescribed to 104 (62.7%) patients. Post-analgesic mean residual pain score was 5.0+/-1.8. Out of them, 47.1% patients waited to be given more analgesia. There was significant less prescription of analgesic to patients with acute abdomen (52.9%) as compared to trauma patients (26.5%) (p=0.001). Overall, more than half of the patients (59.6%) were not satisfied with the pre-operative analgesic treatment in the A&E department."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Pre-operative analgesia was underprescribed and pain was undertreated in accident and emergency department resulting in more than half patients being not satisfied."
        }
      ]},
